# SITEPM long-term architecture

SITEPM is a **builder-first digital toolbag** backed by a **construction operating system**. Complexity belongs inside SITEPM, not on the builder’s desk. The builder provides construction intelligence; SITEPM provides digital leverage. Digital literacy is not a prerequisite for value. Language is one part of the Builder–Technology Gap, not the whole problem. Builder-first does **not** mean field-worker-only: SITEPM remains an AI project manager / construction OS for contractors; Toolbag and Field Intelligence extend it to where work originates. SITEPM scales with the builder, not away from the builder.

Models are replaceable. Durable value is trusted construction/business/property state, history, permissions, provenance, workflows, APIs, and operational memory produced from real work — not transcription or report generation. Future agents operate SITEPM through identity, tenant/project scopes, audit, and human approval for sensitive actions. AI interpretation is never authorization. SITEPM must not substitute for licensed professionals, qualified supervision, inspectors, or safety procedures.

Answers should distinguish documented fact, derived inference, general construction knowledge, regulatory citations, and uncertainty, and should cite evidence when practical. Role-appropriate interfaces are presentations over the same authorized state (SOURCE → FACT → DERIVATION → PRESENTATION).

Knowledge classes are separate: Project, Property, Company, Construction, Regulatory. Language is an overlay (work in your language; common construction meaning underneath) — not a source of truth. Translation may later include field ↔ PM, craft ↔ structured data, and client intent ↔ scope — not only Spanish ↔ English. Regulatory knowledge stays edition- and jurisdiction-specific (California-first, nationally expandable).

Solo builders/tradespeople are first-class potential buyers. The 3–25 person / office-PM contractor remains an important MVP segment. Contractor maturity levels and Construction Spectrum are product-depth models, not pricing and not MVP. Voice/vehicle-first, Field-to-Office, two-way field intelligence, Jobsite Copilot, and Digital Toolbag capabilities are long-term UX, not current implementation.

The live MVP is the wedge. Sequence: Projects → Tasks → Field Logs → Documents → Ask SITEPM/Intelligence. **Ask Stage 3 is implemented (server-only model over Stage 2 evidence; no PDF contents). Ask Stage 4 has not started.** Architecture breadth is not MVP breadth. Do not expand scope to match future surfaces (Property schema, document intelligence, construction/regulatory corpora, bilingual engines, voice OS, Field-to-Office Pipeline, Jobsite Copilot, Estimate, communications, Service, robotics, ingest APIs) unless `SITEPM_BUILD_SPEC.md` and `PROGRESS.md` say so.

Keep Postgres + company-scoped RLS. Do not introduce a graph database, weaken RLS, add service-role to the client, scrape external datasets, or pool tenant-private data. BIM/IFC may inform relationship design; do not turn SITEPM into BIM software or copy external schemas blindly.

Canonical product true north: `docs/PRODUCT_ARCHITECTURE.md`. Also: `docs/DATA_INGESTION_ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/DATA_SOURCE_REGISTRY.md`.

External sources are not reusable until licensing is verified. Publicly viewable does not mean reusable.

Living Property Record lifecycle/handoff requirements are canonical in `docs/PRODUCT_ARCHITECTURE.md` §20. Capture during construction; preserve source → inference → answer, claim-level chronology/authority, service/replacement history and permissioned handoff. `docs/reference-projects/001/` is an offline fictional benchmark, not runtime seed data. Keep its evaluator ground truth outside retrieval. Do not infer implementation authorization from these documents; accepted Stage 3 and existing auth/RLS/Storage boundaries remain unchanged.
